<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heresy</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as heretic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heresy is any belief or theory strongly at variance with established beliefs or customs, particularly the accepted beliefs or religious law of a religious organisation; its proponent is a heretic. The word comes from the Greek haíresis, choice. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> Irenaeus gave it wide currency in his 2nd-century Against Heresies, and its first known legal use came in 380 with the Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a>, which made Christianity the state church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscillian">Priscillian</a>, condemned in 386, was the first Christian heretic to be executed. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, Christianity and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> heresy has been met with censure ranging from excommunication to death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heresy.jpg" alt="Heresy" width="330" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Byzantine Icon-Painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Nicholas_of_Myra_slapping_Arius_at_the_Council_of_Nicaea_Greek_Icon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The English word comes from the Ancient Greek haíresis, which meant a choice or thing chosen, then the school or party of a person's choice, and the process by which a young person examined philosophies to decide how to live. In the early Church a divisive person, warned twice according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a> 3:10, became a technical term for a heretic who promoted dissension. Irenaeus gave the word wide currency in his 2nd-century tract Against Heresies, which called the community's beliefs orthodox and the teachings of the Gnostics heretical, and invoked apostolic succession in support.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a>, to end the debate started by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arius">Arius</a>, called the first ecumenical council and then enforced its orthodoxy by imperial authority. The first known legal use of the term was in the Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> of 380, which made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> the state church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and so overlapped state and church powers of enforcement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscillian">Priscillian</a>, condemned by Roman officials in 386 for sorcery and put to death with four or five followers, was the first Christian heretic executed, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose of Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-siricius">Pope Siricius</a> excommunicated his accusers. Before the 11th century heresies tended to centre on individual preachers or small sects such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a>, Pelagianism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatism">Donatism</a>, Marcionism and Montanism.</p><p>From the 11th century organised groups appeared in the towns of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, among them the Waldensians and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a>, against whom the Church launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Cathar Crusade</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a>. Heresy was a main justification for the Inquisition. Galileo Galilei was found vehemently suspect of heresy and spent his remaining life under house arrest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the Reformation Protestant churches also executed those they judged heretics: Michael Servetus was declared a heretic by both the Reformed and Catholic Churches for rejecting the Trinity. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> about sixty heretics, mainly Protestants, were executed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> and around 290 were burned under Mary I between 1555 and 1558. The last execution for heresy in England was in 1612, and the last known heretic executed by sentence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> was the Spanish schoolmaster Cayetano Ripoll in 1826.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> heretics were from medieval times called zindiqs, a charge punishable by death, and in Judaism Orthodox groups regard departures from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a>'s thirteen principles as heretical. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>'s nearest concept is the tīrthika, a follower of other paths, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> classifies schools as āstika or nāstika by their acceptance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heresy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>R. I. Moore argues in The War on <b>Heresy</b> that it was largely contrived in the imaginations of churchmen from scattered local anticlericalism and a broader spiritual revival, and some scholars have called the fear of Cathars a moral panic comparable with the witch-hunts.</blockquote><a href="/religion/catharism">Catharism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
